        url="https://incometaxindia.gov.in",
        text="India has two income-tax regimes. The new regime is the default: lower slab rates, a standard deduction of ₹75,000 for salaried people, and a Section 87A rebate that makes tax zero for taxable income up to ₹12 lakh (so up to about ₹12.75 lakh of salary), but it allows almost no deductions like 80C, 80D or HRA. The old regime has higher slab rates but lets you claim deductions: 80C (₹1.5 lakh), 80D health insurance, HRA, home-loan interest, NPS extra ₹50,000 and more. A simple rule: if your total deductions are small (typically below ₹3–4 lakh), the new regime usually wins; if you have a big home loan, rent with HRA and many investments, run both numbers. Salaried people can switch every year; use the Tax tab in Artha Kavach to compare both regimes on your salary.",
